Wiring Description:

The system wiring is based on a simplified circuit, using direct connections and resistor-assisted connections between the Arduino board and auxiliary components.
The optical sensors consist of two components:

- An Infrared LED (IR LED)
- A Phototransistor (FT)

These are connected to digital pins D2 and D3, as well as 5V and GND, and operate using the INPUT_PULLUP configuration
The warning LEDs are connected to pins D5 and D6 through approximately 680Ω resistors, which limit current and protect the LEDs.
The IR LEDs are powered from the 5V pin through 220Ω resistors, connected individually in series with each IR LED.
All components share a common ground (GND) to ensure stable system operation.

The chosen wire color coding was implemented to allow quick identification of connections during assembly and maintenance.

The Arduino board operates at 5V, and can be powered using:

- A computer USB port;
- A phone charger;
- Another regulated 5V power source.

Additional red LEDs may be added to the system, either in parallel or in series, provided that the appropriate voltage, current requirements, and resistor values are respected.


