System Architecture and Code Functionality:

The railway level crossing system is built around an Arduino Nano board, which acts as the central control unit. It reads information from the sensors and controls the warning LEDs according to the current system state.
The operation is organized as a state machine, meaning a logical mechanism through which the system transitions from one state to another depending on events. In this case, there are three main states:

- Idle state
- Activation state
- Deactivation state

This structure is particularly suitable for an automation system, as it enables a controlled and predictable response to signals received from the sensors.
The Arduino code represents the core of the system. It defines the system behavior and determines how it reacts to sensor input while controlling the warning LEDs.
The code was written in C++, tested using online Arduino simulators, and later implemented on the website, where it can be easily copied by anyone interested in using it.

Pin Definition:
At the beginning of the code, variables are declared to define the pins used by the system:

- Two pins for the sensors
- Two pins for the red signal LEDs

This stage is important because it ensures clear organization of hardware connections and allows easy modifications if changes are required in the future.

Initial Configuration in setup():

The setup() function runs only once when the system starts.
Here, the pin modes are configured:

- The sensors are set as inputs with internal pull-up resistors;
- The LEDs are configured as outputs.

Serial communication is also initialized at this stage, allowing status messages to be displayed in the Arduino IDE Serial Monitor.
These messages serve a diagnostic purpose, helping monitor the operating state of the system during execution.

Sensor Debouncing:
In a real circuit, mechanical contacts or sensor signals may generate small fluctuations (noise) during switching.
To avoid false readings, the code includes a debouncing function.
This function verifies whether the signal change is stable and ignores very short fluctuations.
As a result, the system achieves more reliable and accurate sensor readings.

System Activation:

When one of the sensors detects a train, the system enters the activation state.
At this moment:

- The triggering sensor is stored;
- The warning lights are activated;
- LED brightness values are reset;
- Alternating operation between the red LEDs begins.

This logic closely simulates the behavior of a real railway level crossing, where warning signals alert road users of an approaching train.

Red Signal LED Operation:
The red LEDs do not switch on and off abruptly. Instead, they use a fade effect, meaning their brightness gradually increases and decreases.
This effect is achieved using PWM (Pulse Width Modulation), a method through which Arduino controls the apparent brightness of an LED by rapidly varying the time it remains powered.
At any given moment, one LED is active while its brightness gradually decreases, after which the second LED takes over.

This alternating behavior creates a highly realistic simulation of an actual railway crossing warning system.

System Deactivation:
The system does not stop immediately after the train is no longer detected.
A 2-second safety delay is implemented after the opposite sensor is released, preventing false deactivations (for example, due to larger gaps between train wagons).
If the sensor becomes active again during this interval, the system continues operating without interruption.
This logic is particularly important because it better replicates the behavior of real railway automation systems.

Safety Timeout:
The code also includes a general timeout mechanism, which automatically deactivates the system if it remains active for too long.
This safety measure helps prevent system lockups or unintended continuous operation in unforeseen situations.


